================================================================================================================
PART 3 -- THE CLOSURE EQUATION IN CLOSED FORM, and how much margin the kill has.
================================================================================================================
  GATE-G (THEOREM B, condensation front outside nothing we measure):  3 c_s^2(1)/p  >  L v_c^2,
         with L = ln(r_ref/r_in).  So   c_s^2(1)  >  (p/3) L v_c^2.
  GATE-L (Lyman-alpha at z = 3, a = 1/4):                            c_s^2(1) 4^-p  <  eps_Lya.
  Eliminating c_s^2(1):

         4^p / p   >   L v_c^2 / (3 eps_Lya).

  The left side is monotone increasing for p > 1/ln4, so this defines a MINIMUM p.  THEOREM A caps p at
  3.  The kill is the statement p_req > 3.  Both sides are measured, not assumed:

      v_c (km/s) r_in (kpc)  Lya tol  eps_Lya = c_s^2(1/4)  p_required  vs ceiling 3
             300         10     0.90             5.042e-08        3.53 CLOSED by 0.53
             300          1     0.90             5.778e-08        3.71 CLOSED by 0.71
             300         10     0.96             2.680e-08        4.09 CLOSED by 1.09
             250         10     0.90             4.336e-08        3.33 CLOSED by 0.33
             250         10     0.96             2.339e-08        3.89 CLOSED by 0.89
             180         10     0.90             3.271e-08        2.98  OPEN by 0.02
             180         10     0.96             1.814e-08        3.53 CLOSED by 0.53
             300         30     0.80             7.619e-08        2.95  OPEN by 0.05

      READ THIS ROW BY ROW, NOT AS A HEADLINE.  Every row that both (i) includes the deepest MOND-regime
      galaxies (v_c ~ 250-300 km/s: NGC 2841, NGC 5055, UGC 2885 and their kin are all in SPARC and all
      sit on the RAR) and (ii) uses the data-calibrated Lyman-alpha tolerance, closes -- but the margin
      is 0.53 to 1.09 in the exponent, i.e. a factor 2.1 to 4.5 in c_s^2, NOT orders of magnitude.
      And two rows land marginally on the OPEN side: restricting the galaxy gate to Milky-Way-sized
      potentials at the loose tolerance, and the deliberately over-generous 30 kpc / 20 percent row.
      This is a NARROW closure.  It is a real one -- it survives every tightening -- but it is not a
      theorem-strength kill, and it must not be quoted as one.
  [PASS] CLOSE-1  the closure equation is 4^p/p > L v_c^2/(3 eps_Lya), and at the fiducial (generous) gate settings it requires p > 3.5 for the deepest MOND-regime galaxies -- rising to about 4 at the data-calibrated Lyman-alpha tolerance -- against THEOREM A's hard ceiling p < 3.  The barotropic branch misses, but by a factor of only 2 to 4 in c_s^2, not by orders of magnitude   (p_required = 3.53 (generous) and 4.09 (data-calibrated) vs ceiling 3 -- a shortfall of 2.1x to 4.5x in c_s^2)
  [PASS] CLOSE-2  and the kill's SENSITIVITY is reported rather than hidden, INCLUDING the settings under which it fails to close.  Tightening the Lyman-alpha tolerance to its data-calibrated value or shrinking the tolerated condensation radius strengthens it; restricting the galaxy gate to Milky-Way-sized potentials at the loose tolerance brings it marginally OPEN.  The kill therefore rests on one physical input -- that the dark fluid must be smooth in v_c ~ 250-300 km/s spirals too -- which is exactly the population the L61 gate was built on, but it is a single point of failure   (range of p_required across the 8 settings: 2.95 to 4.09; the ceiling 3 lies INSIDE that range, so the closure is setting-dependent and is reported as narrow)

================================================================================================================
PART 4 -- COULD MOND ITSELF RESCUE THE LYMAN-ALPHA LEG?  Quantified.
================================================================================================================
  In the hybrid the baryons feel MOND, so one might hope a boosted baryon growth refills the z = 3 power
  the fluid erased.  The required boost is computable: if the fluid leaves a fraction f of the CDM power,
  the baryon linear growth must be enhanced by 1/sqrt(f) in delta on that scale, at that redshift.

          p   c_s(1)   P/P_CDM (k=8, z=3)  required growth boost in delta
        0.0      207              0.00474                            14.5
        1.0      300              0.15384                             2.5
        2.0      500              0.47519                             1.5
        2.9      900              0.67624                             1.2
  [PASS] MOND-1  the MOND escape hatch is quantified and closed at linear order.  At the galaxy-gate minimum with a constant sound speed the fluid leaves ~1 percent of the CDM power at k = 8 Mpc^-1, z = 3, so the baryons would need their linear growth enhanced by an order of magnitude in delta on 100 kpc PHYSICAL scales at z = 3 -- where accelerations are far above a0 and MOND is OFF.  A MOND boost of the linear growth is at most a factor of a few, not ten   (required delta-boost 15x at k=8 Mpc^-1, z=3; the programme's own g04h reached the same conclusion independently (P(k) not regenerated at linear order))

      Honest caveat, carried forward: this is a LINEAR statement.  A nonlinear top-down fragmentation
      channel in a MOND cosmology is uncomputed here and remains the one place the Lyman-alpha leg could
      in principle be softened.  It is the same uncomputed channel the programme already has on file.

